# Generated contract artifacts and their source schemas are always LF so the
# Row 5 drift gate (regenerate + `git diff --exit-code`) is byte-stable across
# operating systems and every contributor's core.autocrlf setting. The Pydantic
# exporter and scripts/gen-contracts.mjs both emit LF; these attributes make the
# stored blob and every checkout match.
schemas/*.schema.json text eol=lf
frontend/src/contracts/*.schema.json text eol=lf
frontend/src/contracts/*.d.ts text eol=lf

# The baked glyph manifest and its source SVGs are LF too, so the glyph bake is
# byte-stable across operating systems (test_glyphs.py compares the committed
# manifest to a fresh bake).
frontend/public/assets/glyphs/*.json text eol=lf
assets/glyphs/*.svg text eol=lf

# The lexicon fixtures are byte-exact slices of their raw sources, several of
# which use CRLF. Any end-of-line conversion would rewrite those bytes and break
# the row-4 claim that a fixture is a contiguous slice of its source, so git must
# store and check them out unchanged.
datasets/fixtures/lexicon/** -text

# The golden fixtures are the opposite case: they are hand-written, ASCII-only
# (Tamil is escaped) and LF-only, and a test byte-compares one against a fresh
# render. `-text` would leave them at the mercy of whatever the author's editor
# wrote, so they are pinned to LF instead - the same rule the generated contract
# artifacts above use, and for the same reason.
datasets/fixtures/*.jsonl text eol=lf

# The authored lexicon source is hand-written UTF-8 Tamil whose sha256 and byte
# count are recorded in config/lexicon-sources.json and re-verified by EXTRACT on
# every run. Any end-of-line conversion would change those bytes and fail the
# run on the next contributor's machine, so LF is pinned rather than left to
# core.autocrlf.
datasets/lexicon/sources/llm-authored/entries.jsonl text eol=lf

# The published lexicon and its index. Every file's sha256 and byte count are
# recorded in lexicon.meta.json and re-checked against disk by the row-11
# Oracles, and "re-publishing an unchanged store is byte-identical" is the whole
# gate - so a checkout that rewrote the line endings would fail on whoever
# cloned it rather than on the change that broke it. The publisher writes LF
# explicitly; these rules make git store and check out the same bytes.
datasets/lexicon/by-class/*.ndjson text eol=lf
datasets/lexicon/lexicon.meta.json text eol=lf

# The committed expectation the fixture pipeline is byte-compared against, for
# exactly the same reason: it is the output of the same writer at fixture scale.
datasets/fixtures/lexicon-expected/** text eol=lf
